59,3,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,Minako,,"Option A is the most stable. It eliminates the need for constant auditing and complex calculations, while ensuring a fair, fixed contribution. It solves the fuel disparity by making costs predictable and the scheduling stress by providing clear ownership.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Option A is certainly the most elegant solution. By removing the need for constant negotiation and meticulous tracking, we eliminate the very frictions that sour these arrangements. It replaces a ledger of grievances with a simple, predictable rhythm.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Option A provides predictability, which is a start. However, if someone holds the car on their day and leaves it empty or dirty, the next person still suffers. We must agree that ""ownership"" of a day includes a duty of care.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shion,,"Option A is far too simplistic. It ignores actual usage and allows people to avoid paying for the distance they drive. A fixed share is just another form of guesswork; it's not logical, it's just convenient.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think Option A sounds the best. If we have our own days, we wouldn't have to spend every evening planning and arguing about who gets the car. It would just be... quiet.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Minako,,"@Shion, while Option B is more precise, the administrative burden would be immense. We've already seen that tracking every run creates tension. Option A provides a fair baseline that prevents any one person from being a silent subsidizer.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Shion, your pursuit of ""cold logic"" often overlooks the human cost. Option B is a recipe for the very auditing and friction I've warned against. Why trade a small financial imbalance for a permanent state of surveillance?","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Shion, precision doesn't guarantee reliability. Option B requires constant recording, which is just more administrative stress. I prefer Option A, provided we establish a strict rule that the car must be clean and fueled for the next person.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Asuka, @Benjamin, you're both prioritizing comfort over correctness. Option B is the only one that ensures the person who uses the car most pays the most. Anything else is just a polite way of being unfair.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Asuka"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I'm sorry, but Option B sounds like it would just bring back all the stress. Recording every single trip and calculating distances... wouldn't that just mean more arguing and more time spent on the ledger?","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Minako,,"@Shion, correctness is useless if the system is too burdensome to maintain. Option A ensures everyone contributes equally to the fixed costs and fuel, removing the resentment of subsidization without the exhaustion of constant tracking.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Shion, you mistake precision for justice. A system that requires us to monitor each other's every kilometer is not a solution; it is a surveillance state. Let us choose the peace of Option A over the pedantry of Option B.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Shion, your focus on ""correctness"" ignores the reality that the car is a tool, not a math problem. Option A is the most practical way to ensure the car is available when I need it, as long as we enforce a handover standard.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Benjamin, calling it ""pedantry"" doesn't change the fact that Option A is logically flawed. It rewards the lazy and penalizes the frequent user. If we can't handle the truth of the numbers, we're just pretending to be fair.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I can't handle the numbers anymore. Option B just feels like more of the same stress we've been having. I really want us to stop fighting, and Option A seems like the only way to finally have some peace.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Minako,,"It is clear that Option A is the most sustainable choice. It balances financial equity with mental well-being, preventing subsidization without the friction of auditing. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,I concur. Option A preserves our sanity and our friendship by removing the catalyst for conflict. Let us choose a predictable rhythm over a perpetual audit. I vote for Option A.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Asuka,,"I agree. Option A is the most reliable for my schedule and reduces administrative friction. As long as we commit to a strict handover standard for cleanliness and fuel, it is the best choice. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shion,,"You're all choosing convenience over integrity. Option A is a compromise of logic that allows unfairness to persist under the guise of ""peace."" I refuse to support a system based on a lie. I vote for Option B.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Daisuke,,I... I don't want to fight anymore. I just want to be able to relax without worrying about the schedule every night. Option A is the only one that feels peaceful. I vote for Option A.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
